# ai.wing — change log & architectural record

Running log of every significant structural change, feature addition and
architectural decision made on this project. Newest entries at the top.
Each entry is written so the change can be traced, reviewed or reverted.

---

## [1.0.0] — Mobile pass, repository, deployment readiness

### The site had no mobile navigation at all

`nav[aria-label="Primary"]` was `hidden lg:block`. Below 1024px — every
phone, every tablet — the Plans / Hardware / How it works / FAQ links did not
exist in any form. Nothing pointed at it because the anchors still worked if
you knew to scroll; it simply could not be navigated.

Added a disclosure menu in the header:

- a 44×44 toggle carrying `aria-expanded` / `aria-controls`
- a dropdown, not a modal — the rest of the page stays operable, and there is
  no focus trap to get wrong
- Escape closes it; so does scrolling, because a panel anchored to the header
  should not trail the page as the header leaves
- the four anchors at 48px each, plus the full phone number as a fifth row,
  which is also where the number goes now that the bar collapses it

Desktop nav and the menu button are mutually exclusive, asserted rather than
assumed: `exactlyOneNavMechanism` is checked at 768px and 1440px.

### The header could not fit a 320px bar with a menu button added

Wordmark 191px + phone button 146px + a new 44px button overflows a 390px
screen before padding. Rather than shrink everything, the phone button
collapses to an icon below 640px with the number moved into the menu, and the
"Authorized Retailer" eyebrow appears only from 420px. Measured at 320px:
header content 224px against 320px available.

### Touch targets and type

| | before | after |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Targets under 24px (390px viewport) | **18** | **0** |
| Smallest long-form text | 10px | 11px |
| Card fine print | 11px | 12px |

The footer links were the bulk of it at 16px tall; they now carry a real row
height. The only text left under 11px is three short bold uppercase badge
labels — "Free", "Popular", "Limited-time offer" — where it is conventional
and legible.

### Animation on phones

Four ambient washes across eleven sections is 44 continuously animating
composited layers. Fine on a desktop GPU, wasteful on a mid-range phone where
it competes with scrolling for the same budget. Below 640px the two
supporting washes are dropped: **42 layers in the DOM, 22 animating**. The
effect still reads.

Also added a `@media (hover: none)` rule to suppress card lift transforms, so
a tap does not leave a card stuck in its hover state.

Pointer-driven effects were already gated on `pointerType === 'mouse'`, and
Lenis already leaves touch scrolling native.

### Verified across widths

| | 320 | 390 | 768 | 1440 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Horizontal overflow | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| True leaks | 0 | 0 | 0 | 0 |
| Targets under 24px | 0 | 0 | 0 | — |
| Nav mechanism | menu | menu | menu | desktop |

19/19 `tel:` links keep `data-call-cta` at every width; body CTAs remain
exactly `{"Call to order", "Call for pricing"}`.

### Dead code removed

`TiltCard.tsx` and `Parallax.tsx` had zero references after the hero rebuild
and the move away from 3D card tilt. Deleted rather than shipped.

### Repository and deployment

Pushed to `https://github.com/PavithraEbbali/sparklight`, `main` at `a1c4d9f`,
51 files. `.next`, `node_modules` and `.claude/` are ignored.

Vercel needs no configuration: framework auto-detects, **no environment
variables**, defaults are correct. Verified against a real production build
rather than the dev server, since the two differ on image optimization:

```
clean next build       -> / prerendered as static
next start, GET /      -> HTTP 200, 316 KB, 98 ms
/_next/image (hero)    -> HTTP 200, image/jpeg, 35 KB
```

CTAs, the disclosure and `og:image` are all present in the server-rendered
HTML, so the page is complete before any JavaScript runs.

### Still outstanding

The one thing unchanged since 0.3.0: **no scroll-triggered behaviour has been
observed running.** The Browser pane is a hidden page — `document.hidden` is
true, `requestAnimationFrame` never fires, IntersectionObserver delivers
nothing — so wipes, staggers, text reveals, the hero cascade and lazy image
loading are all verified structurally and none has been watched. Everything
in this log rests on DOM geometry, computed styles and HTTP responses. A
single scroll in a normal browser window is the outstanding check.

---

## [0.4.4] — Fix: the card photograph was showing 7% of the frame

The 0.4.3 card looked wrong on sight — a blurred slab of wall with the phone
nowhere in it. The cause was not the crop position, it was the size of the
box `object-cover` was solving against.

The image was given `absolute inset-0`, so its box was **the whole card**.
Measured at 337×778, which is portrait, against a 1253×768 landscape source:

```
cover scale        1.01
rendered frame     1265 x 776
horizontal crop    930px discarded  ->  26% of width survives
visible band       top 208px of 776  ->  27% of height
frame reaching the screen            ~7%
```

Seven per cent of the photograph, taken from the out-of-focus background
wall. The subject was never within a thousand pixels of the viewport.

**Fix: bound the image box to the 208px band rather than the card.** Cover
then solves 446×208 against 1253×768 — nearly the source aspect — so almost
nothing is thrown away:

| | before | after (desktop) | after (320px) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Image box | 337×778 (whole card) | 446×208 (band) | 286×208 |
| Horizontal crop | 930px | **0px** | 0px |
| Frame visible | **~7%** | **76%** | **84%** |

The gradient moved with it and can go back to percentages, because unlike
the card the band has a fixed height. The card's own background is now plain
white — with the photo bounded, the `bg-surface` it carried underneath was
never visible.

### The general lesson

`object-cover` does not crop toward the visible area — it crops toward the
box it is given. Handing a landscape photograph a portrait box discards the
width silently and there is no warning anywhere; it simply renders and looks
bad. **When a photo only shows in part of an element, size the image box to
that part, not to the element.**

### One thing left unresolved

`currentSrc` reports the `3840w` candidate for a 446px box, despite a correct
`sizes` and a DPR of 1 — `640w` is the right answer. This appears to be the
hidden pane resolving `sizes` before layout, since the image never actually
loads there, so it could not be confirmed either way. It is bounded in any
case: the source is 1253px, Next never upscales, so the ceiling is the full
file at 60.7KB on a lazy below-fold image. Worth a glance in a real browser's
network panel, not worth engineering around from here.

### Verification

`tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`, `next build` clean. Copy still begins at 208px on
solid white. At 320px: band 286×208, 84% of the frame visible,
`scrollWidth === 320`, 0 overflow, **0 true leaks**, 8 images, 19/19 `tel:`
links keep `data-call-cta`.

---

## [0.4.3] — Home phone: photograph as the card, card centred

Third image mode added. `imageMode` is now `'side' | 'background' | 'card'`;
the phone section uses `card`.

### How the photo sits on the card

A plan card carries a lot of small text — name, blurb, price line, four
features, three fine-print rows, CTA. A tint flat enough to keep all of that
legible would leave almost nothing of the picture showing, so a uniform
overlay was never going to work. Measured: copy directly on this photograph
is **1.70:1**.

So the photo is given the top of the card to itself and the copy sits below
it, with a gradient handling the join. The picture reads at full strength for
the first ~140px, fades, and is solid white by 200px. The content begins at
208px, on solid ground: **12.16:1**.

The card is centred in the section, and the heading block above it is centred
too — a lone centred card under a left-aligned heading reads as a mistake
rather than a decision.

### Two bugs found by measuring rather than looking

**1. The gradient stops had to be pixels, not percentages.** A percentage
gradient is relative to card height, so its opaque point drifts with the
amount of copy — on a taller card the 50% `via` stop lands *below* where the
text starts, putting the first lines on a半-transparent band. Fixed stops at
138px / 182px / 200px are independent of card height.

**2. `sm:p-7` silently beat `pt-52`.** The first implementation appended
`pt-52` to the existing `p-6 sm:p-7`. Measured result: `padding-top: 28px`,
with the heading sitting at 29px — directly on the photograph — at every
width from 640px up. `sm:p-7` lives inside a media query, so it wins over an
unprefixed `pt-52` regardless of class order. The photo variant now composes
its own padding (`px-6 pb-6 pt-52 sm:px-7 sm:pb-7`), leaving padding-top
uncontested. Re-measured: 208px, heading at 209px.

This is the kind of thing that looks fine in a screenshot at one width and is
broken at another. Worth remembering: **appending a `pt-*` to a `p-*` that
has a responsive variant does not do what it looks like it does.**

### Verification

`tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`, `next build` clean. Phone section holds exactly one
image and it is the card's own background; no side frame. At 320px: card
288px wide and centred, `padding-top` 208px, heading at 209px,
`scrollWidth === 320`, 0 overflow, **0 true leaks**, 8 images, 19/19 `tel:`
links keep `data-call-cta`, body CTAs still exactly `{"Call to order",
"Call for pricing"}`.

> Repeat offence: the shell mangled another `node -e` edit containing nested
> template literals, silently dropping the grid-centring class. Caught by
> checking the reported flags, then fixed with the editor. A damage grep for
> `key={}` / `className={}` / `${}` now runs after any scripted TSX edit. The
> lesson from 0.4.2 was written down and not followed — scripted edits to TSX
> with template literals are simply not worth it.

---

## [0.4.2] — Headline highlight

The hero headline is the largest element on the page and was carrying no
brand at all — plain ink, no accent. It now gets a **gold sweep behind the
key phrase**, which is the accent language Sparklight already uses on its own
site (the yellow marks around their hero).

### Why a sweep behind the words rather than coloured type

Gradient or coloured headline text was the obvious alternative and is the
wrong tool here. The gradient would have to run purple → gold to read as
Sparklight, and gold type on white is about **1.6:1** — the second half of
every highlighted word would be effectively invisible. A sweep painted
*behind* the words leaves the type at `--color-ink` and its contrast
untouched.

Measured on the live page: ink on `gold-300` is **9.01:1** against a 3.0
requirement for large text.

### Implementation

`TextReveal` gained an optional `highlight` prop taking a phrase, matched on
word boundaries against `text`. A phrase that does not match renders no
highlight rather than throwing or mangling the headline. The phrase lives in
`HERO.h1Highlight` in `lib/content.ts`, so which words are marked is data,
not markup.

The word markup needed restructuring. Each word was previously a single
`overflow-hidden` mask, and an absolutely positioned sweep inside that mask
would have been clipped away by it. Each word is now a `relative` wrapper
holding the sweep and then the mask, so:

- the sweep is `absolute` and an **earlier** sibling
- the mask is `relative`, therefore positioned, therefore painted **above**

Both confirmed in the DOM rather than assumed. The sweep animates
`scaleX 0 → 1` from `originX: 0`, timed to land just after its own word has
finished rising, and is frozen under `prefers-reduced-motion` — which also
required giving the reduced-motion branch the real word markup, since it
previously short-circuited to a bare string.

### Verification

`tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`, `next build` clean. One sweep, on the right phrase.
At 320px: headline wraps to 4 lines at 288px wide, fits the viewport, the
sweep stays inside the headline box, `scrollWidth === 320`, 0 overflow,
**0 true leaks**, 19/19 `tel:` links keep `data-call-cta`.

> Note: two passes of this edit were mangled by the shell — `node -e` with
> nested template literals ate the backticks in `key={\`${word}-${i}\`}` and
> two doc comments. Caught by grepping for the damage rather than by trusting
> the write. Structural edits to TSX containing template literals should use
> the editor, not a shell heredoc.

---

## [0.4.1] — Offer moved onto the photograph; ambient light raised

### The gold bar is off the top

It read as a second navigation strip, and it put the page's strongest
commercial message in its weakest position — a band people scroll past on
the way to the headline. The offer now sits **on the photograph**, anchored
to the foot of the frame and hanging slightly below it.

That does two jobs at once: the offer gets the attention the picture
attracts, and the picture gets the weight the offer carries. They reinforce
each other instead of competing for the top of the page.

The speed chip that used to sit on the photo's bottom-left is gone — the
offer card already states the plan and its speed, so the chip was repeating
itself two inches away. The free-install roundel stays on the top-right
corner.

### Highlighting the image

Two additions, neither of them laid over the picture:

- a brand-light bloom behind the panel (`spark-400 → spark-200 → gold-300`),
  which drifts on the existing `aurora-b` cycle
- a 2px gradient hairline around the frame, drawn as a padded wrapper
  because a `border` cannot carry a gradient

The photograph itself is still completely untouched.

### Ambient light raised

A fourth counter-drifting wash added, peak opacities raised, blobs enlarged,
and the drift amplitude on `aurora-a` and `aurora-b` roughly doubled.

| | before | now |
| --- | --- | --- |
| subtle | 0.13 / 0.12 / 0.09 | 0.16 / 0.17 / 0.13 / 0.11 |
| bold | 0.26 / 0.24 / 0.18 | 0.34 / 0.32 / 0.26 / 0.20 |

**Opacity here is not a free dial**, and the ceiling is set by the smallest
lightest type on the page. At full strength directly over a wash centre,
`--color-mute` at 13px would fall to about 3.4:1.

It does not, because every wash centre sits **outside** its section box — the
blobs are anchored at negative insets, so only the falloff reaches the
content. That was checked rather than assumed: the four gradients were
evaluated analytically at five points on every text element in every
section, compositing each wash over white by its own distance falloff.

| Section | Worst element | Contrast | Needs | Margin |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| hero | 13px CTA note | 5.83 | 4.5 | +1.33 |
| how-it-works | 16px intro | 6.94 | 4.5 | +2.44 |
| plans-bundle | 16px intro | 7.09 | 4.5 | +2.59 |
| *(all 10 sections)* | — | — | — | **all pass** |

Heaviest tint reaching any text: `rgb(247,241,247)`. Raising the washes
further means darkening `--color-mute` first.

### One thing that looked like a bug and was not

At 320px the photo measured `left: 48, right: 332` against a 320px viewport,
apparently 12px past the edge, while `scrollWidth` stayed at 320 and the leak
scan stayed at zero.

Both are true. The wrapper was sitting at `translate(30px, 0)`, `opacity: 0`,
`blur(8px)` — its **un-run entrance state**. Reveals never fire in the hidden
pane, so `initial` persists. At rest the photo sits at 16–304, inside the
gutter. Worth remembering when reading any geometry measured in this pane:
an element inside a `Reveal` is offset by its `distance` until the animation
runs.

### Verification

`tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`, `next build` clean; `/` still static. At 320px:
`scrollWidth === 320`, 0 overflow, **0 true leaks**, the offer card does not
collide with the trust chips, 19/19 `tel:` links keep `data-call-cta`, body
CTAs still exactly `{"Call to order", "Call for pricing"}`.

---

## [0.4.0] — Hero rebuilt as a split; mobile wash made a gradient

### The hero was broken, and my verification missed it

Seen at ~1840px the headline and subline were running straight across the
dark kitchen doorway. The 0.3.1 contrast table said the hero passed, and it
was not lying — it was **measured at a single viewport width**.

`object-cover` re-crops at every width. At 1024px the hero is tall relative
to the frame, the image scales ~1.9×, and `object-left` lands the headline on
the pale wall. At 1840px far less vertical crop is needed, the frame sits
much wider, and the doorway slides under the copy. Same CSS, same photo,
completely different result.

**The lesson is the finding.** Contrast that depends on the browser window
being a particular width is not contrast, and a single-width check cannot
detect that. Any future full-bleed text-on-image needs measuring across a
range, or the layout needs changing so the question cannot arise.

### The fix: the text no longer sits on the photograph at all

The hero is now a split — copy and ordering controls on the left, the
photograph in its own rounded panel on the right. Copy is always on white,
so legibility is fixed at every width by construction rather than by tuning.
As a bonus this is what finally delivers the actual request from 0.3.1: the
picture can be shown **completely untouched**, because nothing needs to be
washed out to protect text sitting on top of it.

Verified at 1440px: `headlineOverPhoto: false`, `sublineOverPhoto: false`.
Geometric, not a colour sample — the rectangles do not intersect, so there is
nothing left to measure.

### The gauge card is gone

`components/HeroVisual.tsx` deleted. Two competing visuals in one hero is
what made it feel cluttered; the card was being clipped at the right edge and
its badge collided with the window frame behind. Its three facts — eero mesh,
unlimited data, no contract — duplicated the metric strip below word for
word, so nothing was lost. What it did contribute is kept as two small
overlays on the photo: the free-install roundel on the top-right corner and a
speed chip on the bottom-left, the latter reading `formatSpeed(lead.speedDown)`
so it still tracks `lib/content.ts`.

Hero height at 1440px: **1047px, down from roughly two full screens.**

The hero photo also gained real alt text; it is content in its own panel now,
not a decorative backdrop, so an empty alt was no longer correct.

### Mobile section: flat tint replaced with a gradient

Asked to reduce the wash. Dropping the flat tint from 72% to 58% put the
intro at **4.03:1** and the eyebrow at **3.33:1** — both under the floor. At
that alpha the darkest blocks in the frame are near-black, so no text colour
reaches 4.5; the flat tint had no workable setting.

A gradient solves it, because the text does not occupy the whole section.
Phone runs top to bottom, `white/90 → white/55 → white/20`; desktop runs
left to right, `white/95 → white/70 → white/5`.

Measured at 390px, using the real gradient alpha across each element's own
vertical span and the darkest 10×10 block in that band:

| | flat 72% (before) | flat 58% (tried) | gradient (now) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Eyebrow | 4.08 | 3.33 ✗ | **7.18** ✓ |
| Heading | 6.13 | 4.03 | **8.04** ✓ |
| Intro | 3.90 | 4.03 ✗ | **5.99** ✓ |
| Wash over the card area | 0.72 | 0.58 | **0.38** |

So the photograph is considerably **more** visible than at either flat
setting — 38% wash across most of the section instead of 72% everywhere —
and every text element reads better at the same time.

### Verification

`tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`, `next build` clean; `/` still static. At 320px:
`scrollWidth === 320`, 0 overflow, **0 true leaks**, headline does not
intersect the photo, 8 images, 19/19 `tel:` links keep `data-call-cta`, body
CTAs still exactly `{"Call to order", "Call for pricing"}`.

