## [0.3.2] — Home phone photograph

The ninth photograph supplied and wired in, closing the gap left open in
0.3.1. Same pipeline as the other eight: the Gemini sparkle watermark sat at
the usual position, so the standard 155px right trim removed it and left a
16:10 frame. **1.93 MB PNG → 54 KB WebP.**

Alt text was written against the delivered frame rather than the prompt, so
it names what is actually there: the keys, the stack of mail and the daylight
from the front door.

Total image payload across all nine: **808 KB**.

### Side alternation reworked

Adding this photo exposed a flaw in how the image side was chosen. It keyed
off `index`, the section's position among **all** sections — including the
two that carry no photograph. With the phone section illustrated, the run
came out `fiber R · tv L · mobile bg · phone L · hardware L · how-it-works R`
— two consecutive lefts.

The side is now derived from position among the sections that actually carry
a **side** photo, ignoring unillustrated ones and the full-bleed mobile
section:

```ts
const sideIndex = ACTIVE_SECTIONS
  .filter((s) => s.image && s.imageMode !== 'background')
  .findIndex((s) => s.line === meta.line);
const imageOnLeft = sideIndex % 2 === 1;
```

Verified at 1165px: `fiber:right  tv:left  mobile:—  phone:right
hardware:left  how-it-works:right`, no image overlapping a heading, no
overflow. This now stays correct on its own if a section gains or loses a
photograph.

Note the earlier check at a 1009px viewport reported every photo as "left".
That was the measurement, not the layout — 1009px is below the `lg`
breakpoint where the header grid collapses to one column, so image and
heading share a left edge by design. Side assertions are only meaningful at
≥1024px.

---

## [0.3.1] — Hero photo unscrimmed, mobile section full-bleed

### Hero: photograph at full strength

All three white scrims removed, and `Aurora` no longer renders in the hero —
the photograph is the background now. No overlay, no veil, no blur.

Removing the wash exposed a real problem that the wash had been hiding.
**The crop anchor, not the overlay, was doing the work.** The hero is tall
and the photograph is wide, so `object-cover` discards a large slice: at a
1024px viewport the image scales 1.9× and roughly half the frame is cropped.
Anchored `object-right`, the pale left wall the picture was composed around
was cropped away entirely and the headline landed on the **dark kitchen
doorway**:

| | `object-right` | `object-left` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Headline, darkest patch | **1.71** ✗ | **4.45** ✓ |

Switching to `object-left` puts the headline back on the empty wall.

The body copy still fell short at 3.49:1, so the three text elements sitting
directly on the photo were darkened rather than re-introducing a scrim —
which keeps the picture fully visible, the point of the change:

| Element | Colour | Darkest bg under it | Contrast | Needs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Headline | `#2E3644` | `rgb(160,158,127)` | **4.45** | 3.0 |
| Subline | `#4C5458` → `#2E3644` | `rgb(175,175,163)` | **5.49** | 4.5 |
| ZIP label | `#656565` → `#2E3644` | `rgb(186,187,182)` | **6.29** | 4.5 |
| CTA note | `#656565` → `#2E3644` | `rgb(196,196,194)` | **6.96** | 4.5 |

All measured against the darkest pixel inside each element's own rectangle,
mapped through the live `object-cover` transform — not against an average.

### Mobile section: photo as the full section background

`ServiceSectionMeta` gained `imageMode: 'side' | 'background'`. The mobile
section is the first to use `background`: the photo covers the section, and
the side frame is not rendered.

Some wash is unavoidable here — unlike the hero there is no empty corner to
put the text on, and the frame is a mid-tone street scene. It is kept as
light as the numbers allow and confined to the reading column:

- **≥1024px** — `white → white/88 → white/20` left to right, so the right of
  the frame is essentially untouched
- **<1024px** — a flat `white/72`, because the text spans the full width

At 72% on a phone the intro measured 3.90:1, under the floor. Rather than
thicken the wash, the eyebrow and intro are darkened in `background` mode
only (`text-spark-600 → 700`, `text-body → text-ink`):

| Element | Before | After | Needs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Eyebrow (phone) | 4.08 ✗ | **5.06** ✓ | 4.5 |
| Intro (phone) | 3.90 ✗ | **5.88** ✓ | 4.5 |
| Heading (phone) | 6.13 ✓ | 6.13 ✓ | 3.0 |
| Heading / intro (desktop) | 9.26 / 5.89 ✓ | unchanged | — |

Plan cards are opaque white, so they were never at risk.

### Home phone section — delivered in 0.3.2

Held open in this revision for want of a photograph. Closed out below.

### Verification

`tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`, `next build` clean; `/` still static. At 320px:
`scrollWidth === 320`, 0 overflow, **0 true leaks**, hero scrim count **0**,
mobile background covers its section, 19/19 `tel:` links keep
`data-call-cta`, body CTAs still exactly `{"Call to order", "Call for
pricing"}`.

