## [0.3.0] — Photography

Eight client-supplied photographs added. Sources were 3–5 MB PNGs, 1408×768,
**28.7 MB** in total.

### Processing

Each source carried Gemini's sparkle watermark roughly 120–135px in from the
right edge (located by scanning the bottom-right quadrant, not by guessing).
Trimming **155px off the right** removes it and lands every frame at ~16:10,
which is the ratio the layout wanted anyway.

All were then re-encoded to WebP q82. The OG card is the exception: it is a
JPEG at exactly 1200×630, because some social scrapers still reject WebP.

**28.7 MB → 0.73 MB**, a 97% reduction, before `next/image` does its own
per-breakpoint resizing on top. Measured in the browser: a 480px-wide slot
receives a 480×294 file, not the full-width source.

### Placement

| Section | Image | Side |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Hero | `hero-home` | full-bleed background |
| Fiber | `fiber-build` | right |
| TV | `tv-livingroom` | left |
| Mobile | `mobile-on-the-go` | right |
| Hardware | `equipment-modem-eero` | left |
| How it works | `install-technician` | right |
| Why order here | `family-connected` | inside the closing CTA panel |
| — | `og-share` | `og:image` + `twitter:card` |

Sides alternate R/L/R/L/R down the page so consecutive illustrated sections
do not read as a repeating template. `components/SectionMedia.tsx` holds the
shared 16:10 frame, the lazy loading and the `sizes` hint.

Paths and alt text live in `IMAGES` in `lib/content.ts`, alongside everything
else — no image path is hard-coded in a layout file.

### Hero contrast, measured

The background photo sits under three stacked white scrims. Rather than
assume the text stayed legible, the composite was computed: the photo was
drawn to a canvas, the **darkest 16×16 block in the entire image** was
found, the scrim alphas were composited over it (using a pessimistic 0.80
for the horizontal wash instead of its actual ~1.0), and the result checked
against the type colours.

| Text | Ratio | WCAG AA | Result |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Headline `#2E3644` | **10.40** | 3.0 (large) | pass |
| Body `#4C5458` | **6.61** | 4.5 | pass |
| Small print `#656565` | **4.99** | 4.5 | pass |

That is worst-case; the real ground under the headline is close to pure
white.

### Other changes

- `themeColor` was still `#27455C`, left over from the pre-purple palette.
  Now `#8D3B90`.
- `twitter:card` added as `summary_large_image`.
- The hero photo is `priority`; everything else is lazy.
- `SectionWipe` split into an observed outer element and an animated inner
  one. Not strictly required, but it keeps the observer off the animated
  node and lets content wipe in over its own background.

### A correction to 0.2.x

Earlier entries blamed blank screenshots and unrevealed sections on "the
pane's capture pipeline". That was half right and I stated it too
confidently. The actual cause was found here:

```
documentHidden: true      visibilityState: "hidden"
requestAnimationFrame:    fires 0 times
IntersectionObserver:     delivers 0 entries — even for a synthetic
                          position:fixed div, even for document.body
```

When the host window is not displayed, Chrome throttles the page completely.
Nothing paints, `rAF` never runs, and IntersectionObserver never delivers.
So every scroll-triggered reveal legitimately stays in its `initial` state
and every lazy image legitimately never loads — in that pane. It is an
observation artefact, not a site defect, but the sections really were
unrevealed rather than merely uncaptured.

Consequence for this log: **no scroll-triggered behaviour on this site has
been observed running.** Section wipes, staggered grids, text reveals, lazy
image loading and the hero entrance cascade are all verified structurally —
correct markup, correct computed styles, assets returning HTTP 200 with real
bytes — but not watched. They need one pass in a normal browser window.

---

## [0.2.3] — Hero visual rebuilt: structure over scatter

Seen at full size the 0.2.2 visual did not hold up. Specific faults:

- the three gold marks (`×`, `+`, arc) sat at arbitrary coordinates with no
  relationship to anything and read as artefacts, not accents
- the fact chips were absolutely positioned at hand-picked percentages, so
  they hung off the left and bottom edges and collided with the dial ring
- the arc gradient ran purple → gold, and the midtones came out **muddy tan**
- a full-circle ring left a large dead void around the readout
- the badge was rotated with rotated text, half off the corner

### The fix: one column, no absolutes

The card is now a **network status card** laid out as a single flex column —
header row, gauge, stat footer. Nothing is absolutely positioned inside it,
so nothing can overlap or bleed past an edge at any width. The decorative
marks and floating chips are gone; their content moved into the stat footer
where it is aligned and legible.

**Gauge.** Replaced the full circle with a 270° arc path opening at the
bottom, which has an honest start and end and no dead void. Geometry derived
rather than eyeballed, then verified numerically:

```
centre (100,100)  r 80  sweep 270°  start θ=225°  end θ=−45°
path   M 43.43 156.57 A 80 80 0 1 1 156.57 156.57
fill   0.82  →  tip θ = 225 − 0.82×270 = 3.6°  →  (179.84, 94.98)
```

The tip marker is placed at that computed point, so it lands **on** the end
of the arc rather than near it.

**Gradient** now runs `#8d3b90 → #c98fcd`, purple to light purple only. Gold
appears exactly twice: the tip marker and the badge. Routing the arc through
gold was what produced the tan.

**Badge** is anchored to overlap the card's top-right corner deliberately,
with a 5px white ring and **horizontal text**. No rotation.

**Motion** is unchanged in principle — entrance settles, depth from pointer
response — but with the scattered layers gone, the parallax became a single
restrained card tilt (±4.5° / ±3.5°) with the badge riding forward.

### Two fixes found by measuring

1. `"Whole-home Wi-Fi"` wrapped to two lines, pushing its column out of step
   with the other two. Labels cut to one word each: Wi-Fi / Data / Contract.
2. `"eero TrueMesh"` then wrapped at 320px (36px tall against 18px for the
   others). Changed to `"eero mesh"` — Sparklight's own wording for it, and
   short enough to hold one line. Verified: all three values 18px.

### Verification

`tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`, `next build` clean; `/` still static. At 320px:
`scrollWidth === 320`, 0 overflow, **0 true leaks**, card 288px inside the
gutter, badge right edge at 312px within the viewport. At 1180px the card
sits inside its grid column with no overflow.

---

## [0.2.2] — Offer card contained, hero motion rebuilt

### The offer strip was reading as a second navbar

Making it full-bleed in 0.2.1 was the wrong call: edge-to-edge with a flat
top and bottom, sitting directly under the header, it looked like chrome
rather than a promotion. It is now a **contained, rounded card** —
`rounded-[1.25rem]`, inside the page gutter, with air above and below, a
soft gold glow and an inset white hairline. It reads as an offer.

### Hero motion: from fidgeting to choreographed

The previous pass had three separate infinite loops running in the hero —
chips bobbing on `float-soft`, the badge rocking on `tilt-badge`, and rings
on `wifi-pulse`. Continuous motion on every element reads as restlessness,
not craft. The principle applied instead:

> **Animate in once, then rest. Depth comes from response, not repetition.**

**Removed:** `float-soft`, `float-soft-lag`, `tilt-badge`, `wifi-pulse`.

**Entrance is now a single choreographed cascade.** The timeline lives in one
`T` constant at the top of `Hero.tsx` so the rhythm can be read and tuned in
one place rather than hunting delays through the markup:

| Beat | s | What |
| --- | --- | --- |
| offer | 0.00 | Offer card resolves out of blur |
| eyebrow | 0.14 | Retailer chip |
| headline | 0.22 | Per-word mask reveal |
| subline | 0.46 | Blur-up |
| zip | 0.58 | ZIP checker |
| cta | 0.68 | Call button |
| chips | 0.78 | Trust chips |
| metrics | 0.90 | Metric strip |

Inside the visual, running alongside: the panel settles (0.10), the dial arc
**draws itself** via `pathLength` 0 → 0.76 (0.42), the readout resolves out
of a 12px blur (0.78), the badge springs in (1.02), and the fact chips
stagger (1.16+). `Reveal` gained a `blur` prop so the cascade resolves out of
softness rather than plainly sliding.

**Depth now comes from pointer parallax.** `HeroVisual` became a client
component that tracks the cursor across the panel and drives three spring
layers at different rates — dial ~8px, accents ~18px, badge and chips ~27px.
Verified live: dispatching a `pointermove` moved the layers to
`translate(6.3, 4.1)` and `translate(14.2, 9.0)` respectively. Mouse pointers
only; touch and `prefers-reduced-motion` get the static composition.

**One loop survives in the hero:** three signal rings on a 5.2s stagger at
0.45 peak opacity, which is the one place repetition means something (a
signal). Page-wide infinite animation inventory is now exactly:
`aurora-a/b/c` (ambient section light), `offer-sheen`, `signal-pulse` ×3,
`marquee-track`. Nothing else repeats.

### Verification

`tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`, `next build` clean; `/` still static. At 320px:
`scrollWidth === 320`, 0 overflow, **0 true leaks**. 19/19 `tel:` links keep
`data-call-cta`. 0 canvases. Settled hero state confirmed in the DOM
(`filter: blur(0px)`, opacity 1, dial `stroke-dasharray: 0.76px 1px`).

