## [0.2.0] — Rebrand to the real Sparklight palette, hero rebuild, motion cull

Driven by a screenshot of the live sparklight.com homepage supplied by the
client, which settled the palette question raised in 0.1.0.

### Palette: purple and gold on white, replacing blue-on-navy

The deviation flagged in 0.1.0 is resolved in favour of the real brand.
Sparklight is **purple and gold**, and its site has no dark theme. The
screenshot confirms the purple utility bar, the purple primary button, and
the gold promotional banner, all on white.

| Token | Hex | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--color-spark-500` | `#8D3B90` | Logo spark glyph, top utility bar, primary button |
| `--color-gold-400` | `#F2C94C` | Offer banner, "FREE INSTALL" badge, accent marks |
| `--color-ink` | `#2E3644` | Headlines |
| `--color-body` | `#4C5458` | Body copy, official logo wordmark |
| `--color-surface` | `#F8F6F9` | Lavender-tinted panel |

`navy-*` and the old `spark-*` blue ramp were deleted outright, not aliased,
so nothing can quietly keep rendering blue. All call sites were migrated and
the tree greps clean of the old tokens.

**Verified: zero dark surfaces.** A DOM sweep computing relative luminance of
every element's background found no surface below a luminance of 60. Body is
`#FFFFFF`; the footer moved from `#070E14` to the light lavender surface and
closes with a purple rule mirroring the top disclosure bar.

### Brand name

`RETAILER.name` is now `Sparklight` (was `Clearline Connect`); zero
occurrences of the old name remain.

> **One thing I changed rather than took literally.** `LEGAL.disclosure` was
> a template over `RETAILER.name`, which would now have rendered
> *"Sparklight is an independent authorized retailer of Sparklight."* It is
> rewritten in the third person: *"This site is operated by an independent
> authorized retailer of Sparklight and is not the Sparklight corporate
> website."* The footer copyright line likewise reads "© Independent
> Authorized Retailer of Sparklight" rather than asserting copyright as
> Sparklight. The persistent top-bar disclosure is unchanged.

### Hero rebuilt

The old "copy left, pricing card right" split was the generic template the
client called out. It now stacks:

1. a highlighted gold offer ribbon carrying the lead promotion, its price
   lockup and a CTA, with a slow sheen sweep
2. the headline at full column width
3. one elevated action bar holding the ZIP checker and the call button
4. trust chips
5. a three-part metric strip

Headline changed from *"Internet that connects your whole home."* to
**"Fiber-powered internet for every room in your home."**

### Motion: removed, kept, added

**Removed** — `FiberField.tsx` (the hero black-hole canvas) and
`NetworkAssembly.tsx` (the scroll-scrubbed network diagram) are deleted, not
just unmounted. The page now renders **zero** `<canvas>` elements and runs no
`requestAnimationFrame` loop of its own.

**Replaced** — `TiltCard` is no longer used by plan cards. Hover is now a 4px
lift with a border tint and a slightly deeper shadow, on `transform`,
`box-shadow` and `border-color` only. Applied consistently to plan cards,
step cards and benefit cards.

**Added** — `components/motion/Aurora.tsx`: soft purple/gold radial washes
drifting on three long offset cycles, behind the hero and every section.
Pure CSS keyframes, no JS, no bundle cost.

> Aurora deliberately carries **no blur filter**. The first cut used
> `blur-3xl`, which put ~34 large offscreen compositing surfaces on the page.
> A radial gradient fading to `transparent` is already soft, so the blur was
> visually redundant and expensive. Removing it changed nothing visible.

Kept: Lenis, section clip-path wipes, text reveals, staggered grids, magnetic
buttons, the marquee.

### Card CTA placement

The call button is now the **last element** in every plan card, after the
fine print, with `mt-auto` pushing it to the bottom edge so buttons line up
across a row regardless of copy length. Verified across all 12 cards: CTA is
the final child in each, sitting a consistent 25–29px (the card padding) from
the bottom.

### Copy made professional

| Was | Now |
| --- | --- |
| Internet that connects your whole home. | Fiber-powered internet for every room in your home. |
| What it actually costs to get connected | Equipment, installation and monthly fees |
| From ZIP code to a working network | Ordering in four steps |
| "Four steps, one phone call. The diagram fills in as you scroll — the fiber drop reaches the gateway…" | "From confirming availability at your address to booking the installation, the whole process is handled on a single call with an authorized retailer." |
| `<title>` "Sparklight Authorized Retailer \| Sparklight" | "Sparklight Authorized Retailer \| Internet, TV, Mobile & Home Phone" |

### Verification

build / `tsc --noEmit` / `eslint` all clean; `/` still prerendered static.
At 320px: `scrollWidth === 320`, not horizontally scrollable, **0 true
leaks**. 19/19 `tel:` links carry `data-call-cta`. Body CTA labels remain
exactly `{"Call to order", "Call for pricing"}`. 0 canvases. 0 dark surfaces.
0 references to the old brand name.

> **Screenshot limitation.** The Browser pane captures the hero correctly but
> returns blank or vertically-offset frames for this page after any scroll.
> Ruled out as page bugs: `getBoundingClientRect` reports the sticky header
> at `top: 0` and every section at its correct offset with `clip-path:
> inset(0%)`; `elementFromPoint` returns the expected element at every probe
> height; and forcing `body { overflow-x: visible }` at runtime changed
> nothing. It is the pane's capture pipeline. Layout was therefore verified
> programmatically. A visual pass on the sections below the hero is still
> worth doing in a normal browser.

