## [0.1.1] — Two fixes found during verification

**`components/ServiceSection.tsx` — single-plan sections left an empty grid cell.**
Mobile and Phone each carry one plan, but their section meta requested two
columns, so each rendered a 438px card beside 438px of dead space. The
requested column count is now capped at the actual plan count
(`Math.min(meta.columns, plans.length)`), and the 1-column case constrains
the grid to `max-w-md`. Measured after the fix: Mobile and Phone render a
single 448px card with no empty cell; every other section is unchanged.
This keeps working automatically if a plan is later added or removed from
`lib/content.ts`.

**`components/motion/FiberField.tsx` — canvas could animate while off screen.**
The wake-up path used `io.takeRecords().length === 0 && start()`, which was
wrong: `takeRecords()` only drains *queued* entries and is almost always
empty, so returning to a visible tab restarted the render loop even when the
hero canvas had been scrolled well out of view. Intersection state is now
tracked in an explicit `onScreen` flag, and a single `sync()` starts the loop
only when the canvas is both on screen **and** in a visible tab. Both the
observer callback and the `visibilitychange` handler route through it.

> Note on verification: the Browser pane reported `document.hidden === true`
> throughout this session (the host window was not displayed), which zeroes
> `requestAnimationFrame` and returns blank screenshots. Structure, geometry,
> computed styles, overflow and DOM content were therefore verified
> programmatically rather than by eye. The canvas was confirmed to paint its
> static frame; live playback of the rAF-driven disc has not been observed on
> screen and is worth a visual pass.

---

## [0.1.0] — Initial build: Sparklight Authorized Retailer landing site

### Scope
Single-page, static-rendered marketing site for an independent authorized
retailer of Sparklight. Strict front-end build: **no backend, no API routes,
no database.** The ZIP checker validates input format and routes the visitor
to a phone call; it never claims to have queried a coverage database.

### Stack
| Layer | Choice | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Framework | Next.js 16.3.5 (App Router, Turbopack) | Static export of a single route; zero client JS for the content itself |
| Language | TypeScript 5 (strict) | The pricing model is the contract; it must be type-enforced |
| Styling | Tailwind CSS v4 (`@theme` tokens) | Brand palette lives in CSS custom properties, not in class soup |
| Animation | `motion` (Framer Motion v12 successor) | Transform/opacity-only animation on the compositor |
| Scrolling | `lenis` | Smooth scroll that still drives native `window.scrollY`, so `useScroll` and anchors keep working |

Route output: `/` is `○ (Static)` — fully prerendered.

---

### Decision 1 — `lib/content.ts` is the single source of truth

Every price, speed, promotional qualifier, equipment fee, feature bullet,
FAQ answer and legal disclosure on the site is exported from this one file.

**Verified:** `grep` across `app/**/*.tsx` and `components/**/*.tsx` returns
**zero** hardcoded prices, speeds or monthly fees.

The cascade is implemented through derived helpers at the bottom of the file
rather than through prop drilling:

| Helper | Drives |
| --- | --- |
| `getPlansByLine(line)` | Which cards render in each service section |
| `ACTIVE_SECTIONS` | Section order **and** omission — a service line with no plans never renders |
| `LEAD_PLAN` | The hero price anchor, resolved from `HERO.leadPlanId` |
| `hasPrice(plan)` | Whether a price lockup or a "pricing by address" line shows |
| `ctaLabel(plan)` | **The only place CTA wording is decided** |
| `formatSpeed` / `speedSummary` | `1000` → `"1 Gig"`, `200/20` → `"200 Mbps down / 20 Mbps up"` |
| `LOWEST_PRICE` | The footer "Plans from $X/mo." summary |

**Cascade test performed and passed.** Changing only `price`/`cents` on two
plans in `lib/content.ts` (1 Gig `40.00 → 35.50`, Lift `29.95 → 24.99`),
with no TSX touched, correctly updated: the hero price anchor, both plan
cards, the recomputed footer "Plans from" line (which switched to the new
lowest price), and the generated SEO meta description. Values were reverted
after the test.

### Decision 2 — One universal `<PriceLockup plan={plan} />`

`components/PriceLockup.tsx` is the only component that renders a price.
It draws a flex row: dollar sign, dominant integer, muted cents stacked over
the billing period. The dominant integer is held inside the required
**2.5rem – 3.5rem** band at all three sizes (`sm` 2.5rem, `md` 3rem,
`lg` 3.5rem).

An unpriced plan is not a broken lockup: `hasPrice()` is false, so it renders
a "Pricing by address" line instead, and `ctaLabel()` independently switches
that card's button to **"Call for pricing"**. Screen readers get a single
`sr-only` string (`"$40.00 per month"`) because the split integer/cents
markup would otherwise be read as `"$4000"`.

### Decision 3 — Service-line audit against sparklight.com

Live audit of sparklight.com, `/internet`, `/fiber`, `/sparklighttv`,
`/phone`, `/internet-mobile-bundle`, `/internet/wifi-equipment`,
`/internet/wifi-equipment/eero`, `/internet-gig` and
`/internet/affordable-internet-plans`.

| Line | Verdict | Basis |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Fiber | **KEPT** | `/fiber` exists. "100% Fiber: in select communities Sparklight delivers internet over a fully fiber-optic network." Nearly $1B invested; symmetrical speeds; multi-gig in select markets. No published fiber-specific rate card → both fiber cards resolve to "Call for pricing". |
| Cable | **KEPT** | "Fiber-Powered Network Plans": 1 Gig, 600 Mbps, 300 Mbps, plus Lift Internet. |
| Bundles | **KEPT** | TV and Mobile both require Sparklight Internet; Internet + Mobile is actively promoted. |
| TV | **KEPT** | Standard Sparklight TV Plus and Economy Sparklight TV Plus. |
| Mobile | **KEPT** | Sparklight Mobile is real: nationwide 5G, up to 6 lines, requires Sparklight Internet. |
| Phone | **KEPT** | Sparklight Phone residential voice; rates are published per service area via a rate card, so no price → "Call for pricing". |

No service line was omitted, because Sparklight sells all six. Had one not
existed, `ACTIVE_SECTIONS` would have dropped it automatically — there are no
empty placeholder sections or "call to ask" filler cards anywhere.

**Pricing actually published by Sparklight, and therefore the only prices
shown:**
- 1 Gig Internet — `$40.00/mo.` promotional, 12 months, new customers, Auto Pay + paperless
- Lift Internet — `$29.95/mo.`, 200/20 Mbps, equipment included, assistance-program verification
- Economy Sparklight TV Plus — from `$54.00/mo.`
- Standard Sparklight TV Plus — from `$148.00/mo.`

300 Mbps, 600 Mbps, both fiber tiers, Mobile and Phone carry **no** invented
price. Sparklight publishes only a `$60–$105/mo.` range for standard internet
rates, which is carried as a `promoQualifier` rather than being split into
fake per-tier numbers. Those cards say "Call for pricing".

### Decision 4 — Brand palette (verified hex, with one deviation flagged)

Hexes sampled from live Sparklight assets rather than guessed:

| Token | Hex | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `--color-spark-500` | `#3860BE` | Accent blue on sparklight.com (`rgb(56,96,190)`) |
| `--color-navy-500` | `#27455C` | Navy panel background on sparklight.com |
| `--color-slate-ink` | `#2E3644` | Inline SVG chrome colour |
| `--color-slate-body` | `#4C5458` | **Sampled from the official logo wordmark** |
| `--color-spark-mark` | `#8D3B90` | **Sampled from the spark glyph in the official logo** |
| white | `#FFFFFF` | — |

> **Flagged deviation.** The brief specified "dominant Sparklight Blue".
> Pixel-sampling the official logo (`Logo.png`) shows Sparklight's current
> mark is actually a **slate-grey wordmark `#4C5458` with a purple spark
> `#8D3B90`** — not blue. Both were kept as real brand colours, and the
> verified site blue `#3860BE` was made the dominant primary as instructed,
> with the purple retained as the accent/spark. Every value above is a real
> Sparklight hex. To make purple dominant instead, swap `--color-spark-500`
> and `--color-spark-mark` in `app/globals.css`; nothing else needs editing.

Typeface: Sparklight uses **Effra**, a licensed face, with **Inter** declared
as its own fallback in their stylesheet. Inter is what we load.

### Decision 5 — Animation system, built against a performance budget

Everything animates transform / opacity / clip-path / `pathLength` only.
No animated layout properties, so nothing triggers reflow.

| Requirement | Implementation |
| --- | --- |
| Lenis smooth scroll | `components/motion/SmoothScroll.tsx` — also intercepts `a[href^="#"]` so anchors ease instead of jumping |
| "Black hole" / fiber-optic background | `components/motion/FiberField.tsx` — canvas accretion disc |
| Scroll-scrubbed assembly | `components/motion/NetworkAssembly.tsx` — home network builds itself, reverses on scroll up |
| Scroll-triggered text reveals | `TextReveal` — per-word mask reveal |
| Parallax depth | `Parallax.tsx` — spring-damped, viewport-relative |
| SVG path draw | `pathLength` scrubbing in `NetworkAssembly` |
| Magnetic buttons | `Magnetic.tsx` — spring-tracked, label lags shell for depth |
| Clip-path wipes | `SectionWipe.tsx` — `inset()` and `polygon()` variants between sections |
| 3D card tilt | `TiltCard.tsx` — rotateX/rotateY + pointer-tracked specular highlight |
| Staggered grid entrance | `StaggerGroup` / `StaggerItem` |
| Infinite marquee | `Marquee.tsx` — pure CSS keyframe, duplicated track translating `-50%` |

Performance safeguards:
- `FiberField` caps DPR at **1.75**, scales particle count to viewport area
  (hard cap 260), and **stops its rAF loop entirely** via `IntersectionObserver`
  when scrolled away and on `visibilitychange`. It costs nothing once the
  visitor is reading plans.
- `prefers-reduced-motion` is honoured in **every** primitive: Lenis does not
  initialise, `FiberField` paints one static frame and exits, the marquee
  stops, and each wrapper returns a plain element with no motion props.
- Pointer-driven effects (magnetic, tilt) ignore non-mouse pointers, so they
  never fire on touch.
- `motion.create()` is never called during render — `components/motion/motionTag.ts`
  exports a module-level map that call sites index directly. Calling a factory
  in a render body would remount the subtree each render and also trips
  `react-hooks/static-components`.

### Decision 6 — Negative constraints, enforced and verified

Automated DOM audit run against the live page. All clear:

- No support-routing copy ("New orders only…", account/billing/outage routing) — **absent**
- Hero retains **both** the ZIP checker and a call button — **present**
- Every CTA outside the header/footer reads exactly `"Call to order"` or `"Call for pricing"` — verified, the set of body CTA labels is exactly those two strings
- No raw phone number as a body CTA label — verified, zero matches
- No competitor bashing, no self-deprecating "ordering channel" copy — **absent**
- No "See Sparklight's official Broadband Facts label →" link — **absent**
- No "Pricing observed [date]" / "Pricing current as of" / "Last reviewed" strings — **absent** (note: the bestfiberinternet.us footer reference *does* carry a date stamp; it was deliberately not copied)
- No "Is this the official Sparklight site?" FAQ — **absent**
- No empty placeholder sections — structurally impossible via `ACTIVE_SECTIONS`

### Decision 7 — Page order

Driven by `ACTIVE_SECTIONS`, not by hand-placed JSX:

1. Top disclosure bar — persistent, no dismiss control (`SiteHeader`)
2. Sticky header — wordmark, anchor nav, solid call button with placeholder number
3. Hero — eyebrow, H1, subline, `<PriceLockup>` anchor, ZIP checker **and** call button, trust chips
4. Trust marquee
5. Fiber → Cable → Bundles → TV → Mobile → Phone
6. Honest fine-print grid (`#hardware`)
7. How it works + Why us (`#how-it-works`, `#why-us`)
8. FAQ (`#faq`)
9. Footer

Footer layout, typography and spacing follow bestfiberinternet.us: deep navy
ground (`#070e14`, their `#001226`), muted `#9FB3C8` body text, brand blurb
beside three uppercase link columns, a "Talk to a human" block carrying the
raw number, then a dense required-disclosures slab.

### Decision 8 — Accessibility

- `TextReveal` keeps the full string in one `sr-only` node and marks the
  per-word spans `aria-hidden`, so the headline is announced once, intact.
- `PriceLockup` exposes `"$40.00 per month"` rather than the split markup.
- FAQ accordion uses real buttons with `aria-expanded` / `aria-controls`.
- Every `section` is `aria-labelledby` a real heading id.
- `maximumScale: 5` — the page stays pinch-zoomable (WCAG 1.4.4).
- Single focus-ring definition in `globals.css`; skip link to `#plans`.
- The decorative canvas is `aria-hidden` + `role="presentation"`.

### Verification performed

| Check | Result |
| --- | --- |
| `npm run build` | Passes. `/` prerendered as static |
| `npx tsc --noEmit` | Clean |
| `npm run lint` | Clean |
| Horizontal overflow at 320px | `documentElement.scrollWidth === 320`, `horizontallyScrollable: false`, **0 true leaks** (every out-of-bounds node is inside a clipping ancestor, not merely caught by the body safety net) |
| `data-call-cta` on every `tel:` link | 19/19 present, 0 missing |
| Body CTA labels | Exactly `{"Call to order", "Call for pricing"}` |
| Forbidden-string scan | 0 violations across 11 patterns |
| Pricing cascade | Passed (see Decision 1) |

### Known placeholders to replace before launch

1. **`PHONE`** in `lib/content.ts` — `(888) 555-0142` is in the reserved
   fictional `555-01xx` range so nothing real is dialled. Replace `display`
   and `href` together.
2. **`RETAILER.name`** — currently `Clearline Connect`. Changing it updates
   the header, footer, `<title>`, meta description and the legal disclosure,
   because `LEGAL.disclosure` is a template literal over `RETAILER.name`.
3. Footer legal hrefs are `#legal-*` anchors pending real policy pages.
4. Footer support hours are illustrative.
